[Event "Sicilian Defence: the Kan itself"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0Jbuvv6d/fM8MXH4Z"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis"]

{ Today we learn the Sicilian Kan. Two quiet pawn moves, c5 then a6, and then you punch straight back at his centre. }
1. e4 { He plants his king pawn in the middle of the board. Meet it from the side: push your c pawn two squares. [%cal Yc7c5] } 1... c5 { Good. A wing pawn that still fights for the centre, and unlike e5 it leaves your own king pawn free to come out later. } (1... e5 { A perfectly good reply too, meeting him nose to nose in the centre and heading for the open games. Today we go sideways instead. } 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 { That pin is the Ruy Lopez, a rich opening with a lifetime of theory behind it. }) (1... e6 { The French Defence, solid chess, though that pawn shuts in your light-squared bishop for a long while. We already keep a whole file for it. } 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 { A real fight for the centre, and a perfectly sound defence. Just not the one we are building here. }) (1... c6 { The Caro-Kann, solid and safe, and it frees the bishop before the pawn locks it in. A good defence, only not today's. } 2. d4 d5 3. e5 { Solid, careful chess from him. The Sicilian throws the sharper punch, and that is the one we want. }) (1... Nc6 { Developing a piece is never wrong, but that knight sits right on the square your c pawn was going to want. } 2. d4 d5 3. e5 { He grabs a big pawn centre, and with your c pawn blocked you have no way left to challenge it. }) 2. Nf3 { He develops the knight and eyes the d4 square. Open the door for your dark-squared bishop: push the pawn to e6. [%cal Ye7e6] } 2... e6 { Right. Pushing to e6 opens the diagonal for your dark-squared bishop, the piece doing real work in this whole line. } (2... Nc6 { Also fine. The knight comes out before you commit the e pawn to anything at all. } 3. Bb5 e6 4. O-O { That invites his bishop to check you on b5. Not bad, just a different road to a similar sort of game. }) (2... d6 { The Najdorf, completely sound and about the deepest well of theory in all of chess. } 3. d4 Nf6 4. Nc3 { A whole book of its own to learn. Our set-up asks you to remember far less. }) (2... g6 { The Dragon fianchetto, a fine and sharp system, just not the quiet plan we are building here. } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 { A sharp, tactical world with a homework list all its own. }) (2... a6 { The right idea, played one move too soon. The centre still needs settling first. } 3. c4 { He grabs a second centre pawn while your own a6 has done nothing yet to stop him. }) 3. d4 { There is his centre pawn. Take it: your c pawn captures on d4. [%cal Yc5d4] } 3... cxd4 { Take it. His centre pawn is yours now, and he has to spend a whole move just getting it back. } (3... d5 { A central strike, but he captures first, and you are left facing a check you would rather not answer. } 4. exd5 exd5 5. Bb5+ { Annoying. That check costs you a tempo, and White is left with a real, lasting edge. }) (3... a6 { The pawn move can wait. Right now his centre pawn on d4 is the only thing worth touching. } 4. d5 { He shoves the pawn straight past you and shuts the door before your pieces get a say. }) (3... Be7 { Developing is fine in general, but with the centre still tense this quiet move lets it go cold. } 4. Nc3 a6 5. Be3 { He keeps his whole pawn centre intact, and your pieces are left cramped behind it. }) 4. Nxd4 { He recaptures with the knight. Now the small pawn move that gives our whole line its name: push to a6. [%cal Ya7a6] } 4... a6 { The Kan. This pawn takes the b5 square away from his knight and bishop, and readies your own pawns to roll forward. [%csl Gb5] } (4... Nc6 { Just as strong by any measure. That is the Taimanov, a cousin system worth its own study one day. } 5. Nc3 Qc7 6. Be3 { Good, flexible chess either way. Our version simply keeps the pawn moves easier to remember. }) (4... Nf6 { A natural developing move, and perfectly playable. It just heads toward a different Sicilian than the Kan. } 5. Nc3 Nc6 6. Nxc6 { He trades off the centre knight and gains a move. Fine chess, just not the quiet set-up we are learning here. }) (4... Qb6 { The queen looks bold on b6, but this early she just becomes a target while he calmly keeps developing. } 5. Be3 a6 6. Nd2 { He tucks the knight in safely and keeps building. The queen's early trip has not troubled him at all. }) (4... d6 { Solid and safe, though this pawn locks your own dark-squared bishop in behind it for the moment. } 5. c4 Nf6 6. Nc3 { Now he sets up the full double pawn clamp with nothing at all left to stop him. }) 5. Bd3 { His bishop swings out toward your kingside. Meet it with your own: bishop to c5, eyeing his knight and f2. [%cal Yf8c5] } 5... Bc5 { Your bishop leans on his knight and stares straight down the diagonal at f2. He cannot just ignore it. [%cal Gc5d4,Gc5f2] } (5... Nf6 { A completely natural developing move, just as playable, though the bishop wants that diagonal for itself first in our plan. } 6. O-O d6 7. c4 { All perfectly fine, though he claims the extra central space a move sooner than in our line. }) (5... Qc7 { A classic Sicilian square for the queen, safe and useful, eyeing all the way down toward his king. } 6. O-O b6 7. Re1 { Perfectly sound chess. Our move order simply develops the bishop before the queen. }) (5... Ne7 { A good home for the knight too, with g6 waiting as its very next stop. } 6. O-O Nbc6 7. c3 { Also perfectly fine chess. We just prefer sending the bishop to c5 first. }) (5... Be7 { Playable, though quiet. On e7 the bishop only watches the centre; on c5 it actually bites into it. } 6. O-O d6 7. c4 { Fine chess from him. He simply gets to build his own centre a touch more comfortably. }) 6. Nb3 { He kicks the knight over to attack your bishop. Tuck it right into the corner: bishop to a7. [%cal Yc5a7] } 6... Ba7 { Into the corner. From a7 the bishop still aims at f2, and for now nothing on the board can reach it. [%csl Ga7] } (6... Be7 { Retreating home is safe, but it gives up the whole diagonal the bishop was just fighting to control. } 7. Qg4 g6 8. O-O { His queen gets a free jab at your kingside pawns before you can even get your own king castled. }) (6... Bb6 { Almost the right idea. A7 is the one square deeper in the corner that nothing at all can trouble. } 7. Nc3 d6 8. Bd2 { On b6 his pieces can still find ways to needle the bishop later; on a7 they never will. }) (6... Bb4+ { A check, but it only helps him. He simply blocks with the knight he wanted to develop anyway. } 7. Nc3 Nc6 8. Qg4 { And now his queen is eyeing your undefended g pawn. Not a happy way to spend a check. }) (6... Bf8 { All the way back home. Two whole moves spent just to undo the bishop's own trip out. } 7. c4 Nc6 8. Nc3 { Two moves for nothing, and he spent that time building his clamp on the centre instead. }) 7. Qe2 { His queen steps up to defend the e pawn. Free your other bishop with the small pawn move to d6. [%cal Yd7d6] } 7... d6 { Right. The pawn to d6 finally opens the road home for your light-squared bishop too. } (7... Ne7 { That same knight route from before works just as well here, still heading for g6. } 8. Be3 O-O 9. Nc3 { Sound chess either way. Our line simply frees the bishop with the pawn move first. }) (7... Nc6 { Just as good. The knight has a fine home on c6 and adds more pressure on d4. } 8. Be3 Nge7 9. Nc3 { Good chess all the same. Our move order just opens the bishop's diagonal a move sooner. }) (7... e5 { It grabs central space, but it leaves a permanent hole behind it on d5. } 8. Be3 Bxe3 9. Qxe3 { He plants a knight on that d5 hole, and it may never have to leave again. }) (7... Qc7 { The queen steps out before the position is even settled, and she gets in her own king's way. } 8. Qg4 Kf8 9. Qe2 { Your king cannot castle now, and he has lost nothing at all for the tempo he spent. }) 8. O-O { He tucks his king away into safety. Bring your last piece out: the knight to f6. [%cal Yg8f6] } 8... Nf6 { Your last piece joins in. The knight eyes e4 and stands ready to help push e5. } (8... Nc6 { Just as good. Both knights want work, and this one adds more pressure on d4. } 9. Be3 Nf6 10. c4 { The exact same plan and the exact same position, just reached in a different order. }) (8... Bd7 { A quiet developing move, and perfectly sound chess in its own right. } 9. Be3 Bxe3 10. Qxe3 { Fine either way, though the knight on f6 was the piece with somewhere urgent to be. }) (8... e5 { Too soon. That hole on d5 opens up before your last knight has even come out. } 9. Be3 Bxe3 10. Qxe3 { He aims a knight straight at that hole, and now nothing of yours can contest it. }) (8... Nd7 { The wrong knight for the job. It blocks your own light-squared bishop on its home diagonal. } 9. c4 Ngf6 10. Nc3 { You bring the other knight to f6 anyway, just one slow move later than you needed to. }) 9. c4 { He plays a second pawn into the middle to clamp it. Answer at once: push your pawn to e5. [%cal Ye6e5] } 9... e5 { The centre is yours and every piece has found a real job. That is the whole point of the Kan. [%csl Gd4,Ge5] } (9... O-O { Sensible and safe. Castling is never a wasted move. } 10. Be3 Bb6 11. Nc3 { Also perfectly fine. Our line simply grabs the centre with the pawn first. }) (9... Nc6 { Just as good. The knight adds its own eye on the e5 square too. } 10. Be3 O-O 11. Nc3 { Sound chess. We prefer staking the centre with the pawn move first. }) (9... Nbd7 { Fine as well. That knight is quietly aiming for the e5 square. } 10. Nc3 Ne5 11. Be3 { Also good chess, though the pawn push claims that square faster and for free. }) (9... Qc7 { Playable, but it leaves the fight for the centre completely on hold. } 10. Nc3 Nbd7 11. Be3 { He settles in comfortably, and you still have to find the e5 push anyway. }) 0-1


[Event "Sicilian Defence: the big clamp"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0Jbuvv6d/wspKSn5v"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/1p1p1ppp/p3p3/8/3NP3/8/PPP2PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 0 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/1p1p1ppp/p3p3/8/3NP3/8/PPP2PPP/RNBQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_0_5"]

{ You have just played the Kan's little pawn to a6. This time he answers with a second pawn beside it, on c4. }
5. c4 { Two pawns stand side by side on c4 and e4, squeezing your whole position. This is the bind. How do you start breaking it? [%csl Rd5] } 5... Nf6 { Right. Poke at his e pawn first with the knight, before he finishes building the rest of the clamp. [%cal Gf6e4] } (5... Bb4+ { A check, but it only helps him. He simply blocks, and the blocking knight lands on a genuinely useful square. } 6. Nd2 Ne7 7. Nc2 { You spent a whole move on the check, and his knight ended up better placed than it started. }) (5... b6 { Too slow for this position. It lets him finish building the whole clamp in complete peace. } 6. f3 Nf6 7. Nc3 { Now both his pawns are set in stone, and you never even got the chance to poke at them. }) (5... Qc7 { A nice square for the queen in general, but right now the centre bind is the real emergency. } 6. Nc3 Nf6 7. a3 { He is perfectly comfortable, and your position is still cramped behind those two pawns. }) (5... Nc6 { Trading knights here only helps him. It lets his pawn fill the middle instead of leaving it open. } 6. Nc3 Qc7 7. Be3 { The bind only gets stronger this way, never weaker. }) 6. Nc3 { He guards the e pawn with the knight. Pin that knight to something, so the guard stops really counting. } 6... Bb4 { Your bishop pins the knight to his king, so the e pawn is only guarded on paper now. [%cal Gb4c3,Gf6e4] } (6... d5 { The right break, but at the wrong moment. Without the pin in first, he simply captures on d5 twice. } 7. cxd5 exd5 8. exd5 { He wins the pawn straight back. Retaking with the knight next keeps the material level; the pin first was simply the tidier move order. }) (6... Qc7 { Quiet and safe, but it does nothing about the bind. Those two pawns just sit there untouched. } 7. a3 b6 8. Be3 { He shuts the door on the queenside too, and you never managed to force the central break at all. }) (6... d6 { Solid, but it locks in your own bishop and makes the central break much harder to force later. } 7. Be2 Be7 8. g4 { He grabs space on the kingside as well, now that nothing of yours is pressing back on him. }) (6... Qa5 { The queen pins from a distance too, but with nothing behind her she is easy to chase away. } 7. Bd3 Nc6 8. Nb3 { She has to run for safety, and he gets a free move of development while she does. }) 7. Qd3 { He props the e pawn up with the queen. Strike at the centre right away, before he adds another guard. } 7... d5 { There it is. The pawn to d5 cracks the bind open before he has finished bringing pieces up to defend it. [%cal Gd5e4,Gd5c4] } (7... d6 { One square short of the real break, and the moment to hit d5 with force is already gone. } 8. Nc2 Bxc3+ 9. Qxc3 { He untangles his pieces calmly, and the bind holds firm right through your check. }) (7... O-O { Safe enough, but it lets him slam the door on d5 with a pawn push of his own. } 8. e5 Ne8 9. Be2 { Your knight gets shoved all the way to the edge of the board, and the central break is dead for good. }) (7... Nc6 { Trading into the very pawn you are trying to break only helps him keep it sitting there. } 8. Nxc6 dxc6 9. Qxd8+ { Your queens leave the board together, and your own king gets stuck in the centre with no way left to castle. }) 8. exd5 { He captures on d5. Take back with the pawn, so the centre stays full and every piece keeps a job. } 8... exd5 { Beautiful. His clamp is gone completely, and every one of your pieces finally has room to breathe. [%csl Gd5] } (8... O-O { Careful, that pawn on d5 is simply hanging, and he is more than happy to just keep it for free. } 9. dxe6 fxe6 10. Bg5 { A clean pawn down now, and recapturing with the f pawn has left your kingside full of holes. }) (8... Nbd7 { Too slow. While you develop elsewhere, the pawn on d5 quietly walks away instead of coming home. } 9. Nc2 Nc5 10. Qd4 { You are a pawn light with nothing at all to show for it. }) (8... Bxc3+ { Trading the bishop off first only helps him. It repairs his pawn structure right when it needed the help. } 9. bxc3 O-O 10. dxe6 { And now the d5 pawn is simply gone too, as well as the one you already handed back. }) 9. Be2 { He finishes developing quietly. Get your own king safe, and the whole plan is complete. } 9... O-O { Safe at last, with room for every piece to breathe. The clamp that used to squeeze you off the board is simply gone. } (9... Nc6 { Also completely fine. The knight adds more pressure on d4 before you even think about castling. } 10. cxd5 Nxd5 11. Nxc6 { Equal chess, and pretty lively too, though castling first keeps things a shade tidier. }) (9... dxc4 { That hands the whole centre straight back to him, and all you get in return is a single extra pawn. } 10. Qxc4 Bxc3+ 11. Qxc3 { His queen settles comfortably in the middle of the board, and nothing of yours is pressing on her yet. }) (9... h6 { A wasted breath. Nothing is threatening you, and your own king is still waiting in the middle. } 10. Qe3+ Be7 11. cxd5 { A check first costs you a move, and the pawn on d5 goes along with it. }) 0-1


