{ Same spot again, but this time he just develops his knight. Remember what your Kan pawn on a6 was getting ready for. }
5. Nc3 { A normal developing move from him. Your little a pawn was already preparing something on the queenside. What was it? } 5... b5 { Yes! Your pawns roll forward on the queenside and grab real room there. [%cal Gb5b4,Gb5c4] } (5... Qc7 { A natural square for the queen too, and a perfectly fine move in its own right. } 6. Qf3 Nf6 7. Be2 { Solid, but a6 was played specifically to clear the way for the b pawn to push. }) (5... Nc6 { Good and completely normal chess as well. } 6. Be3 Nf6 7. a3 { Fine chess, though the pawn push to b5 is our own idea for this square. }) (5... Bb4 { The pin looks tempting, but his queen swings out to f3 and starts adding real pressure. } 6. Qf3 d5 7. exd5 { He is doing fine there, and once the centre opens your bishop has nothing left to grab hold of. }) (5... d6 { Solid, but slow. Now he answers by charging forward on the kingside instead. } 6. g4 b5 7. a3 { You push the pawn there anyway, just one move later and a little worse off for the delay. }) 6. Bd3 { He develops his bishop toward your king. One of your own pieces has been waiting for that very diagonal, just from the other end — which one? } 6... Bb7 { Your bishop stares all the way down the long diagonal at his e pawn. [%cal Gb7e4] } (6... Qb6 { Just as good, and it leans on his knight from a useful square too. } 7. Nf3 Qc7 8. O-O { Also fine — that bishop reaching the same powerful diagonal is still our whole plan here. }) (6... d6 { Slow. It lets him strike at your queenside pawns before they have even started rolling. } 7. a4 b4 8. Na2 { The march gets broken up before it really begins. }) (6... Qc7 { Sound enough, though the bishop was the piece with somewhere more urgent to be. } 7. O-O Nc6 8. Nxc6 { He trades pieces and you fall a full move behind in the race to develop. }) (6... Ne7 { That knight heads the wrong way while your queenside pawns just sit there waiting. } 7. a4 b4 8. Nce2 { He cracks your queenside open before your pieces are ready to defend it. }) 7. O-O { His king is tucked away safely now. Bring a knight out and lean on the one he has sitting in the centre. } 7... Nc6 { A knight comes out, leaning on his centre before anything else gets decided. } (7... b4 { Pushing the pawn on is just as good here. } 8. Na4 Nc6 9. c3 { Also strong. The knight joins the attack before you even need to push the pawn again. }) (7... Qb6 { The queen only crowds her own pieces on this square. } 8. Nb3 Qc7 9. Re1 { Nothing was gained by it, and the knight would have done more good on c6. }) (7... Bd6 { The bishop does want that square eventually, but the knight belongs there first. } 8. Re1 Nc6 9. Nxc6 { The same trade happens anyway, and you have simply lost a whole move getting there. }) (7... h6 { A quiet little breath, and he uses the time to take over the centre instead. } 8. Re1 Nc6 9. Nxc6 { You bring the knight out anyway, just one slow move later than you needed to. }) (7... Qc7 { A good Sicilian square for the queen, but the knight was in more of a hurry to get out. } 8. Re1 Be7 9. e5 { Playable, though his central pawn gets rolling forward first. }) (7... d6 { Careful — your b pawn has no guard on it yet. } 8. a4 bxa4 9. Rxa4 { The queenside comes under fire from his rook at once. }) (7... Ne7 { A slow route for the knight. It wants c6, and this is the long way round to get there. } 8. f4 Nec6 9. Nb3 { Two whole moves just to reach the square the other knight route finds in one. }) 8. Nxc6 { He trades on c6. Careful now — which piece should take back? [%csl Rb7,Rd7] } 8... Bxc6 { The bishop, of course. Recapturing this way keeps the long diagonal alive for it. [%cal Gc6e4] } (8... dxc6 { The pawn recaptures instead, and your beautiful bishop ends up buried behind its own pawns. } 9. e5 Qc7 10. Re1 { The diagonal is shut for good, and he owns the whole centre now. }) 0-1


