{ You have played e6 in our Kan, and this time he does not touch the centre at all. He has a slow, quiet plan instead. }
3. d3 { One little step instead of two. He has left the centre completely empty — take it. } 3... d5 { Exactly. He tiptoed, so you march. Two pawns of yours face just the one of his. [%csl Gd5,Ge6] } (3... Nf6 { Fine as well, though the pawn push was the bolder answer to his timid move. } 4. e5 Nd5 5. c4 { He kicks your knight away and grabs the very space you were trying to claim. }) (3... Nc6 { Good and normal, and you get to push the pawn to d5 next move anyway. } 4. g3 d5 5. Qe2 { The same house gets built, just reached one move later than before. }) (3... Be7 { Slow. This gives him the chance to grab the centre with a real pawn after all. } 4. d4 d5 5. Nc3 { Now the whole centre belongs to him, and you never got the chance to contest it. }) (3... d6 { One square short of the real break, and that is exactly the square he wants your pawn stuck on. } 4. c3 d5 5. Qe2 { You push the pawn there anyway, only now you have wasted a whole move getting there. }) 4. Qe2 { His queen tucks in behind the pawn. This is the set-up for a slow kingside build, not an attack yet. } 4... Be7 { Calm and correct. Get the bishop out and your king ready to tuck away safely. } (4... Nf6 { Just as good — a natural developing move that reaches a similar sort of position. } 5. g3 Be7 6. Bg2 { The very same position, just reached through a different door. }) (4... Ne7 { That knight blocks your own bishop's diagonal from coming out. } 5. g3 g6 6. Bg2 { You will need two more moves just to untangle what that one move tied up. }) (4... Qa5+ { A check with nothing at all backing it up. } 5. Nbd2 Be7 6. g3 { He simply blocks it and develops a piece. You played the bishop out anyway, just later. }) (4... Nc6 { Careful — his queen and pawn are both aimed straight at your d pawn. } 5. exd5 Qxd5 6. Nc3 { She has to run from the attack, and he gets a free move of development while she does. }) 5. g3 { He builds his little house in order: pawn, then bishop, then castle. Keep developing to match him. } 5... Nf6 { Right on schedule — your last knight joins the party too. } (5... b5 { Good too — it grabs space on the queenside instead. } 6. Bg2 Nf6 7. O-O { Also strong. The knight comes out first, then the castle follows just the same. }) (5... Nc6 { Also fine — the other knight comes out just as naturally. } 6. Bg2 Nf6 7. a4 { The very same set-up. Knights before pawns is simply the easier rule to remember. }) (5... a6 { A useful little move, but nothing is actually attacking you yet to justify it here. } 6. Bg2 Nc6 7. a4 { He gets a full step ahead in development while you spend the move on that pawn. }) (5... b6 { The bishop does want that fianchetto diagonal, but the knight was quicker to move. } 6. Bg2 Nc6 7. O-O { Playable, though it reaches the same kind of position a little more slowly. }) 6. Bg2 { His bishop settles on the long diagonal aimed at your pawn on d5. [%cal Rg2d5] } 6... O-O { Perfect. Every piece is out, your king is tucked in, and the centre belongs to you. [%csl Gd5,Ge6] } (6... b5 { Just as good — it grabs room on the queenside before castling. } 7. Ne5 Bb7 8. O-O { Also equal. Castling first is simply the calmer road to the same kind of game. }) (6... Nc6 { Fine as well — the knight comes out and eyes the centre before you commit your king. } 7. a4 O-O 8. O-O { You castle anyway a move later, having lost nothing for the detour. }) (6... a6 { A quiet little breath while your own king still waits in the middle. } 7. O-O Nc6 8. Bf4 { He gets there first on every good square while your king sits uncastled. }) (6... h6 { Nothing at all is threatening you right now. Get your king safely in instead. } 7. O-O O-O 8. c4 { A wasted move against a plan this slow — nothing needed defending yet. }) 0-1


[Event "Sicilian Defence: he props with c3"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0Jbuvv6d/E3h09pnY"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pp1ppppp/8/2p5/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 2"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pp1ppppp/8/2p5/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKBNR_w_KQkq_-_0_2"]

{ You have answered with c5 against the Kan. Now a little pawn move from him that hides a big plan. }
2. c3 { He wants to build a full pawn duo on d4 and e4. Stop that plan before it ever gets started. } 2... d5 { Strike straight at his e pawn. His own c pawn means the knight cannot defend it from c3, so it is simply loose. [%cal Gd5e4] } (2... Nf6 { Playable too, but this pawn just gets chased around the board by his e pawn instead of winning anything. } 3. e5 Nd5 4. Nf3 { You get pushed back, and he still builds his big centre anyway. }) (2... Nc6 { Fine, and it lets him build the centre pawns first instead of stopping them. } 3. d4 d5 4. dxc5 { The pawn push here comes just one move too late to stop him. }) (2... e5 { It stops d4 for now, but it hands him the d5 square forever as a home for his pieces. } 3. Nf3 Nc6 4. Bc4 { His bishop is already staring at f7, the weak point beside your king. }) (2... e6 { Solid, though taking on d5 now would have given you a livelier, faster sort of game. } 3. Nf3 d5 4. exd5 { You push the pawn anyway, only now with far less punch behind it. }) 3. exd5 { He captures the pawn. Look at his knight on b1 before choosing how to recapture. [%csl Rb1] } 3... Qxd5 { The queen recaptures, and here she is completely safe — his own c3 pawn blocks the knight from chasing her. [%csl Rc3] } (3... Nf6 { Chasing the pawn back with the knight lets his queen jump out with check instead. } 4. Qa4+ Bd7 5. Qb3 { He simply keeps the pawn while your queen goes chasing after it for nothing. }) (3... Bf5 { Developing first is fine, but the pawn he grabbed is still sitting safely in his pocket. } 4. Qb3 Nf6 5. Nf3 { He grabs at b7 with the queen and stays a pawn ahead the whole time. }) (3... g6 { Too slow here, and the pawn quietly walks away while you were not looking. } 4. Bc4 Bg7 5. Nf3 { You end up simply a whole pawn down, with nothing at all to show for it. }) 4. d4 { There is the centre pawn duo he wanted. Bring a knight out and let it eye the centre for now. } 4... Nf6 { A natural developing move, and it already eyes his centre pawn on d4. } (4... Nc6 { Just as good — this knight comes out and eyes the very same centre pawn. } 5. Nf3 Nf6 6. Be2 { The exact same position as our main line, just with the knights taking their turns in the other order. }) (4... cxd4 { You may take on d4 here, but keeping the tension one more move is worth just as much. } 5. cxd4 e5 6. Nf3 { He gets the free knight square on f3 that you were trying to deny him. }) (4... Bf5 { A fine square for the bishop, though the knight had somewhere more urgent to be first. } 5. Nf3 Nc6 6. Be3 { Playable, just a shade slower to reach the position we actually want. }) (4... e6 { Also sound, and it is a move we play the very next turn in our main line anyway. } 5. Nf3 Nc6 6. Be3 { The same house, reached through a different door this time. }) (4... g6 { A fianchetto set-up, and just as good a plan as the direct approach. } 5. Nf3 Bg7 6. Nbd2 { A fine game either way. Our version simply points the pieces straight at his lonely pawn. }) 5. Nf3 { He develops his knight. Open the road for your bishop and give your queen a guard at the same time. } 5... e6 { It opens a road for your bishop and props up the queen sitting on d5. } (5... Nc6 { Just as good — the knight develops first and the bishop can follow. } 6. Be2 cxd4 7. cxd4 { Fine chess this way too. Ours simply keeps the pawn shape we already know. }) (5... cxd4 { Trading on d4 this early lets his knight jump into the game with extra force. } 6. cxd4 e5 7. Nc3 { His knight arrives on c3 with tempo, hitting your queen and gaining a free move. }) (5... Bg4 { A real pin on the knight, but he can simply break it and hit your c pawn in return. } 6. Nbd2 Nc6 7. dxc5 { He wins the pawn back in a flurry of trades and development. }) (5... Bf5 { A nice square for the bishop, but the pawn move to e6 was the more useful choice right now. } 6. Be2 e6 7. Na3 { His knight finds a fine outpost on c4 while your bishop sits on the rim. }) 6. Be2 { A quiet developing move from him. Now ask a real question of his centre pawn. } 6... cxd4 { Trade it off. Once the knight recaptures, his pawn on d4 stands completely alone. } (6... Nc6 { Every bit as good — the knight joins the game before you even resolve the central tension. } 7. O-O cxd4 8. Nxd4 { You end up trading anyway, just one move later than in the main line. }) (6... Be7 { Fine as well, and the pawn trade was really the whole point of that move. } 7. O-O cxd4 8. cxd4 { The same position again, reached this time by a slightly different road. }) (6... a6 { A wasted breath here. His centre pawn, not the queenside, is the real target. } 7. O-O cxd4 8. Nxd4 { He gets a free move of development out of the delay. }) 7. cxd4 { He recaptures with the knight, and his pawn on d4 now stands completely alone. Finish developing around it. [%csl Gd4] } 7... Be7 { Every piece has found a home, and his lonely pawn on d4 is the weak point to nibble away at. } (7... Qd6 { Just as good — the queen simply steps out of the pin's way instead. } 8. Nc3 Nc6 9. O-O { Also fine. The bishop move was simply the more natural one to make first. }) (7... Nc6 { Also equal here — the knight comes out instead, and the bishop can wait its turn. } 8. O-O Qd8 9. Nc3 { The queen has to step aside soon anyway, so this just does it a move early. }) (7... Qa5+ { A check that only helps him finish developing while blocking it. } 8. Nc3 Be7 9. a3 { He blocks with real gain, and you played the bishop out anyway, just a move later than planned. }) 0-1


[Event "Sicilian Defence: the pawn he throws at you"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0Jbuvv6d/GQc1LTR8"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pp1ppppp/8/2p5/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 2"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pp1ppppp/8/2p5/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKBNR_w_KQkq_-_0_2"]

{ He grabs the whole centre in one go. Do not be shy about punishing it — this is the Kan meeting the Smith-Morra head on. }
2. d4 { Straight into the middle. Take the free pawn he just gave away. } 2... cxd4 { Of course. He has handed over a centre pawn for nothing, and taking it is simply correct. } (2... e6 { That pawn was there for the taking! Instead it runs straight past you and grabs space. } 3. d5 Nf6 4. Nc3 { He ends up owning the whole board, and your pieces are squashed into a tiny corner of it. }) (2... d5 { Both pawns are hanging here, but he recaptures yours first and comes out ahead for it. } 3. dxc5 Nf6 4. exd5 { You end up two whole pawns down, and his own pawns are perfectly healthy to boot. }) (2... g6 { He simply pockets the extra pawn and keeps it. } 3. dxc5 Bg7 4. Nc3 { You are up just the one pawn, with nothing at all extra to show for the detour. }) 3. c3 { He offers a second pawn to get every piece out fast. This is the Smith-Morra Gambit. } 3... dxc3 { Take this pawn too. He now has to prove two whole pawns' worth of activity, and careful play says he cannot. } (3... e5 { Declining is playable, but it simply hands the centre back to him for free. } 4. Nf3 Nc6 5. cxd4 { You gave the pawn straight back and gained nothing at all in return for it. }) (3... Nf6 { Also a decline, and now his pawn gets to chase your knight around the board. } 4. e5 Nd5 5. Nf3 { He wins the pawn back with plenty of time left to spare. }) (3... d5 { It looks active, but he simply captures and ends up standing better for it. } 4. exd5 Qxd5 5. cxd4 { His pawns stay perfectly healthy, and yours have gone missing entirely. }) (3... Nc6 { A half-decline. He recaptures and still keeps his full pawn centre intact. } 4. cxd4 d5 5. Nc3 { You end up simply worse here, and not even a pawn ahead to show for the trouble. }) (3... d3 { The famous decline: hand the pawn straight back. Safe, though a little meek for a gambit fight. } 4. Bxd3 Nc6 5. c4 { He never even had to prove the gambit was sound, and now he gets the pawn clamp too. }) 4. Nxc3 { He recaptures with the knight and points it straight at d5. Now start building your own pawn wall. } 4... e6 { The first brick of the wall. It covers d5 and opens the road for your bishop. [%csl Gd5] } (4... Nc6 { Just as good — the knight comes out before you start laying the pawn wall. } 5. Nf3 e6 6. Be2 { The very same wall, just built with the bricks in a different order. }) (4... d6 { Playable, though it lets his bishop reach the good diagonal first. } 5. Bc4 Nc6 6. Nf3 { His bishop is already staring straight at f7, the weak point beside your king. }) (4... a6 { Too slow while his pieces are still pouring out onto the board. } 5. Bf4 e6 6. Nf3 { He simply keeps developing, and the position stays close to level — no real threat has actually landed yet. }) (4... h6 { A wasted move in a gambit like this one, where every single tempo counts. } 5. Nf3 Nc6 6. Be2 { He gets three pieces out to your solitary one, and that lead in development is the real cost. }) 5. Nf3 { Another piece comes out from him. Guard the centre with a knight of your own. } 5... Nc6 { A guard for the centre, and a completely natural developing move. } (5... Be7 { Fine, though the knight guards the centre more usefully than the bishop does. } 6. Bc4 d6 7. Qe2 { He aims every piece he owns straight at your kingside. }) (5... a6 { Still too slow for a gambit position like this one. } 6. Bf4 Nc6 7. Be2 { You bring the knight out anyway, just one move later than you needed to. }) (5... d6 { The pawn move can wait a little. His own centre pawn is the one that is about to charge. } 6. e5 Nc6 7. Bf4 { He rips the centre open before you get the chance to. }) 6. Be2 { A modest little square for the bishop, and he is ready to castle. Finish building your wall. } 6... d6 { The wall is complete: pawns on d6 and e6, a knight already out on c6, and an extra pawn safely banked. [%csl Gd6,Ge6] } (6... Be7 { Just as good — the bishop comes out first and the pawn wall follows right behind it. } 7. O-O d6 8. Qb3 { The same wall again. Our order simply plays the pawn move first. }) (6... Nf6 { Also equal — a natural developing move, and the pawn wall can easily wait its turn. } 7. O-O d6 8. Qb3 { You play the pawn move anyway, just one move later than in the main line. }) (6... Qc7 { The queen steps out a little too early, and his knight jumps in to punish it. } 7. O-O Nf6 8. Nb5 { She has to run for safety, and he gains a free move of development while she does. }) 0-1


