{ He brings a knight out instead of the king's knight this time. Play the very same moves you already know for the Kan. }
2. Nc3 { A different move order from him, but no reason at all to change your plan. What is your first pawn move in this line? } 2... e6 { Just the same as always. The pawn steps up to e6 and clears a road for your dark-squared bishop. } (2... Nc6 { Equally good, and it leads to a completely different kind of game altogether. } 3. Nf3 e5 4. Bc4 { That is the closed Sicilian played with knights first — a rich system, though ours stays simpler. }) (2... a6 { The right idea, just played in the wrong order for this position. } 3. Nf3 e6 4. d4 { You get the pawn move in anyway, just later. Little pawn moves like this one come second for us. }) (2... d6 { Solid, but it locks your own dark-squared bishop in behind its own pawns. } 3. Nge2 e5 4. Nd5 { His knight lands right on that hole, and nothing of yours can ever chase it away. }) (2... g6 { A fine fianchetto set-up, and a whole different course of theory to learn on its own. } 3. Nf3 Nc6 4. d4 { Sharp stuff, and simply not the quiet set-up we are building in this file. }) 3. Nf3 { Now the other knight comes out too. He is still heading for the same big centre. Your move. } 3... a6 { Right again. The little pawn to a6 takes b5 away from him and gets you ready to push. [%csl Gb5] } (3... Nc6 { Good chess, but it steers into a different Sicilian system entirely. } 4. d4 cxd4 5. Nxd4 { Playable, and it opens up a whole different set of plans to learn some other day. }) (3... d6 { Solid, though a little slower to reach an active position. } 4. d4 cxd4 5. Nxd4 { He gets a free attacking swing on the kingside later as a result. }) (3... Nf6 { The pawn simply chases that knight straight back off its square. } 4. e5 Nd5 5. Nxd5 { Your own pawn structure gets damaged for absolutely nothing in return. }) (3... b6 { The bishop does want that long road, but the pawn to a6 needs to come first. } 4. d4 cxd4 5. Nxd4 { That costs you a whole tempo compared with our actual line. }) 4. d4 { There is the central push. You already know exactly what to do about it. } 4... cxd4 { Take it. Your set-up already matches our Kan exactly, just with the pawn to a6 thrown in a move early. } (4... b5 { The pawn fight in the centre was the real emergency, not this queenside push. } 5. d5 Bb7 6. g3 { He shuts the door on the centre, and your bishop ends up biting on solid stone. }) (4... Be7 { Far too slow while the whole centre is still on fire. } 5. Be3 Qc7 6. d5 { He ends up owning the entire board as a result. }) (4... Qc7 { The pawn in the centre cannot wait. The queen move certainly can. } 5. d5 d6 6. Bf4 { The centre belongs to him now, and your pieces are squashed for it. }) 0-1


[Event "Sicilian Defence: he charges with f4"]
