{ The knight is out, and now a big swing on the kingside. This is the Grand Prix Attack against your Kan. }
3. f4 { He wants his pawns rolling straight at your king. But notice what he skipped — nobody is guarding the centre. } 3... d5 { Perfect. He spent his move out on the wing, so you take the centre while it is empty. [%cal Gd5e4] } (3... Nc6 { Equally good, and the central pawn push still comes right after it anyway. } 4. Nf3 d5 5. Bb5 { The very same idea, just arriving one move later than usual. }) (3... Nf6 { That knight walks straight into the pawn storm he is about to launch. } 4. Nf3 Be7 5. d4 { He rolls his pawns forward with nothing to stop him, since you never touched the centre. }) (3... a6 { Too slow against a charge like this. Strike at the centre instead of waiting. } 4. Nf3 b5 5. d4 { He builds his big pawn centre completely for free. }) (3... Nh6 { Odd, but genuinely playable — the central pawn push was always the real point anyway. } 4. Nf3 Nc6 5. Bb5 { You get away with it here, but the central break would have been the cleaner path. }) 4. Nf3 { He develops a piece. Bring one of your own out and lean on the centre before he can roll his pawns forward. } 4... Nc6 { A piece comes out, leaning on the centre before he gets the chance to roll his pawns at you. } (4... d4 { Pushing straight on is also good, if a touch loose for the position. } 5. Bb5+ Nd7 6. Ne2 { Equal chess either way, though the calmer knight move keeps things tidier. }) (4... Be7 { Fine, though the knight guards the centre more usefully than the bishop does here. } 5. d4 Nf6 6. e5 { He gets to push your pieces straight back as a result. }) (4... dxe4 { That trades away your own good central pawn for nothing. } 5. Nxe4 Nc6 6. Bb5 { He is delighted — the centre opens right up for his own pieces to pour through. }) (4... a6 { Still too slow while the centre is hanging in the balance. } 5. d4 cxd4 6. Nxd4 { He solves his own problem completely for free. }) 5. Bb5 { His bishop pins your knight to the king. Careful now — which piece should come out to help it? [%csl Rc6] } 5... Ne7 { The right one. Your king's knight comes to e7 and guards c6 for a second time. [%csl Gc6] } (5... Nf6 { The natural square for it, but he simply trades there and pushes his pawn through. } 6. Bxc6+ bxc6 7. e5 { Your pawns end up wrecked, and the knight has to run for cover. }) (5... d4 { Pushing on here lets him wreck your pawns just the same. } 6. Bxc6+ bxc6 7. Nb1 { He will pick off the weak pawns one at a time later on. }) (5... Bd6 { Developing straight into the coming storm. } 6. d4 Bd7 7. Be3 { He rips the centre open while your king still sits there in the middle. }) (5... dxe4 { Two of his pieces are aiming at your knight on c6, and this move only answers one of them. } 6. Ne5 Bd7 7. Bxc6 { He starts collecting on c6 — once the trades finish, he keeps the better structure for it. }) 6. exd5 { His pawn captures and now attacks your knight directly. Deal with it at once. [%csl Rc6] } 6... exd5 { Take back with the pawn, so the centre stays full and every piece of yours stays guarded. [%csl Gd5] } (6... Nxd5 { Also fine, though it lets him smash your pawn structure in return. } 7. Bxc6+ bxc6 8. Ne5 { Equal material, and a far uglier position to actually have to play. }) (6... Bd7 { Careful — your knight is simply hanging there with nothing guarding it! } 7. dxc6 Nxc6 8. O-O { A whole piece for just one pawn — the knight is not coming back after this. }) (6... Qc7 { The knight on c6 is still sitting right under that pawn, completely unguarded. } 7. dxc6 b6 8. d4 { A whole piece gone, with nothing at all won back in exchange. }) (6... a6 { The pawn on d5 needs dealing with first, before anything else on the board. } 7. Bxc6+ bxc6 8. dxe6 { He wins a pawn outright and cracks your pawn structure wide open too. }) 0-1


