{ This time a quiet little pawn, making a house for his bishop. This is the closed Sicilian against your Kan. }
3. g3 { Slow and solid from him, and once again he has left the centre completely alone. You already know the answer. } 3... d5 { Yes. Every time he plays slowly, you march straight into the centre. [%cal Gd5e4] } (3... Nc6 { Fine as well, and the very same pawn push is still the right move here too. } 4. Nge2 d5 5. exd5 { The same plan, just carried out one move later than usual. }) (3... Be7 { Solid and slow, just like his own set-up. } 4. Nge2 Nc6 5. Bg2 { He gets to finish his little house in complete peace. }) (3... d6 { One square short of the good one, and that is exactly where he wants your pawn to sit. } 4. Nf3 Nc6 5. d4 { Now he gets to take the centre for himself instead of you. }) (3... a6 { Useful on some other day, but the centre is calling for attention right now. } 4. Nf3 d6 5. d4 { And now he owns the whole centre with nobody left to argue with him about it. }) 4. Bg2 { His bishop moves into its new house and looks straight down the diagonal at your pawn. } 4... d4 { Push straight past it. The pawn kicks his knight away and blocks the bishop's diagonal at the same moment. [%csl Rc3][%cal Gd4g1] } (4... dxe4 { Trading here opens the very road you are trying to block for his bishop. } 5. Nxe4 Be7 6. d3 { His bishop now stares right across the board at you, completely unobstructed. }) (4... Nf6 { Natural enough, but his pawn charges forward and shoves you straight back. } 5. e5 Nfd7 6. f4 { He grabs the space, and your knight ends up stuck in the corner. }) (4... Ne7 { Playable, and the central push really was the whole point of the move anyway. } 5. exd5 exd5 6. d3 { Level material, but he gets to feel comfortable in the position now. }) (4... Nc6 { Careful — once the trade happens, his knight comes and lands right on d5. } 5. exd5 exd5 6. Nxd5 { A clean pawn simply goes missing after that. }) 5. Nce2 { His knight shuffles off to the side, and his pieces are all tangled up together. Bring one of yours out instead. } 5... Nc6 { A piece comes out before you commit to building the pawn wall. } (5... e5 { Good too — it builds the very same pawn wall just one move sooner. } 6. f4 Bd6 7. d3 { Just as good. Our order simply brings the knight out first. }) (5... Bd6 { Also strong, and it aims straight at his kingside too. } 6. f4 e5 7. d3 { Fine as well. Pieces before pawns is generally the safer habit to keep. }) (5... d3 { Giving the pawn back to open the lines. Playable, but greedy in the wrong direction for this position. } 6. cxd3 Nc6 7. Nf3 { He untangles his pieces, and the pawn is simply gone for good. }) (5... Be7 { A shade passive, though at least his knights are still stuck for the moment. } 6. d3 Nc6 7. f4 { He gets the chance to sort his tangled pieces out at his own pace. }) 6. d3 { He props everything up with the pawn. Now build the wall of pawns behind your own proud one. } 6... e5 { Two pawns standing abreast, his knight is stuck, and his bishop stares uselessly at solid stone. [%csl Gd4,Ge5] } (6... Bd6 { Just as good — it reaches the same kind of solid, comfortable position. } 7. f4 f6 8. Nf3 { Also comfortable, and the pawn wall really is the cleanest picture to aim for. }) (6... Nf6 { Playable, and the pawn push really was the whole point of that move. } 7. f4 e5 8. Nf3 { You push the pawn there anyway, just one move later than in the main line. }) (6... f6 { It does defend the pawn on e5, but it leaves your own king a little more exposed for it. } 7. f4 Bd6 8. Nf3 { The pawn could simply have gone there by itself, without needing this extra move first. }) (6... a5 { A useful little move on another day, but the centre was calling for your attention. } 7. f4 e5 8. Nf3 { The very same wall, just built one move slower than before. }) 0-1


