{ No help this time. You have played a6 in the Kan and he brings his bishop out. Play the line from memory. }
5. Bd3 { Do you remember which square the dark-squared bishop belongs on here? } 5... Bc5 { That is the one. It leans on his knight and looks straight through to f2. [%cal Gc5d4,Gc5f2] } (5... Nf6 { Playable, but the bishop was the piece with somewhere important to be this move. } 6. O-O d6 7. c4 { He claims the extra space while you are still developing quietly elsewhere. }) (5... Qc7 { A fine Sicilian square for the queen, just not the move order we are recalling here. } 6. O-O b6 7. Re1 { Sound chess, though our line sends the bishop out first. }) (5... Ne7 { Also good, and it is still heading toward g6 from here. } 6. O-O Nbc6 7. c3 { Fine chess as well, though try to recall the bishop move that comes first in our line. }) (5... Be7 { Playable, though quiet. Think about which diagonal actually bites into his position. } 6. O-O d6 7. c4 { He gets to build his set-up in complete comfort as a result. }) 6. Nb3 { He is asking a question of your bishop. Where does it go, and why does that square matter? } 6... Ba7 { Into the corner, the one square that stays completely safe for a very long time. [%csl Ga7] } (6... Be7 { Safe enough, but you give up the whole diagonal the bishop was just using. } 7. Qg4 g6 8. O-O { He gets a free poke at your kingside pawns for it. }) (6... Bb6 { So close — one square further into the corner is the one nothing can ever trouble. } 7. Nc3 d6 8. Bd2 { His pieces can still find ways to come and bother the bishop on that square. }) (6... Bb4+ { A check just for its own sake. Count what it actually costs you. } 7. Nc3 Nc6 8. Qg4 { Your g pawn ends up hanging as a direct result. }) (6... Bf8 { All the way back home. Two whole moves spent for nothing at all. } 7. c4 Nc6 8. Nc3 { While your bishop wandered off and back again, he built his entire clamp. }) 7. Qe2 { His queen comes to e2. Now recall the small pawn move that frees up a much bigger piece. } 7... d6 { Yes. It opens the road home for your other bishop. } (7... Ne7 { Genuinely just as good — it still heads for g6 all the same. } 8. Be3 O-O 9. Nc3 { Sound as well. Our line simply opens the light-squared bishop first. }) (7... Nc6 { Also good — the knight comes first, and everything else follows from there. } 8. Be3 Nge7 9. Nc3 { Fine, though try to recall the pawn move that comes first in our line. }) (7... e5 { It grabs some room, but it leaves behind a hole he will happily make use of. } 8. Be3 Bxe3 9. Qxe3 { A knight lands on that hole and may never need to leave again. }) (7... Qc7 { The queen steps out a little too early for the position. } 8. Qg4 Kf8 9. Qe2 { Your king cannot castle now, and nothing was gained for it. }) 8. O-O { His king has tucked away. Which one of your own pieces is still sitting at home? } 8... Nf6 { Your last piece comes out, ready to help support the push to e5. } (8... Nc6 { Just as good, and this order simply develops the other knight first. } 9. Be3 Nf6 10. c4 { You reach the identical position either way — only the two knights swapped which one moved first. }) (8... Bd7 { Quiet and sound chess in its own right. } 9. Be3 Bxe3 10. Qxe3 { Fine as well, though the knight was the piece with somewhere more urgent to be. }) (8... e5 { Too soon again. That hole on d5 opens up before you are ready for it. } 9. Be3 Bxe3 10. Qxe3 { He aims a knight straight at that hole once it opens. }) (8... Nd7 { The wrong knight for the job, and it shuts your own bishop in behind it. } 9. c4 Ngf6 10. Nc3 { The other knight belonged on that square instead. }) 9. c4 { He clamps the centre with a second pawn. Do you let him keep it? } 9... e5 { Never. The pawn steps up and the centre is shared between you. That is the whole point of this line. [%csl Gd4,Ge5] } (9... O-O { Good and safe. Castling is never a wasted move. } 10. Be3 Bb6 11. Nc3 { Also fine. Our line simply stakes the centre with the pawn right away. }) (9... Nc6 { Every bit as good as the main line here. } 10. Be3 O-O 11. Nc3 { Sound chess. The pawn push was always our own idea for this square. }) (9... Nbd7 { Fine as well, heading the knight toward the e5 square instead. } 10. Nc3 Ne5 11. Be3 { Also good, just a little slower to get there. }) (9... Qc7 { Playable, though it does nothing at all about the pawn clamp. } 10. Nc3 Nbd7 11. Be3 { He settles in comfortably, and you still have to find that central push anyway. }) 0-1
